/** @page oracleora_lost_connectionsguide Lost Connections

    @tableofcontents

    @section ora_lost_connection_handling Handling a Lost Connection

    The connection to the server can be lost while a call is in progress, for example because the session was killed
    on the server (as happens during Oracle RAC rolling patching, where all sessions connected to the node being
    patched are killed), or because of a network or cluster failover event.  The driver handles the error as follows:
    - <b>outside of a transaction</b>: the driver connects to the server again and executes the statement again in the
      new session, after preparing it and binding its values again; values bound as temporary LOBs are created again in
      the new session
    - <b>in a transaction</b>: the transaction is lost; the driver connects to the server again and raises a
      \c DBI:ORACLE:TRANSACTION-ERROR exception whose description includes the original Oracle error; the application
      must roll back and restart the transaction
    - <b>operations that cannot be executed again</b> (ex: commit, rollback, fetching from an open statement, or
      creating a temporary LOB for a bind value): the lost connection error is raised (ex: \c ORA-00028,
      \c ORA-03113, or \c ORA-03135), and the \c Datasource is closed and is opened again on its next use
    - <b>if the connection cannot be made again</b>: the lost connection error is raised, followed by the error raised
      when connecting again (ex: \c ORA-28000 for a locked account), and the \c Datasource is closed and is opened
      again on its next use

    A lost connection always results in an exception if the operation could not be completed; a commit never
    reports success for a transaction that was lost.

    A call ended by the connection's receive timeout (the \c "recv-timeout" option; see @ref ora_options) is reported
    with \c ORA-12609; the statement is not executed again, as it can have run on the server, and a transaction in
    progress is lost.

    @section ora_lost_connection_restart Restarting Transactions

    \c SqlUtil::get_restart_transaction_reason() and \c SqlUtil::RestartableTransaction recognize
    \c DBI:ORACLE:TRANSACTION-ERROR and the Oracle errors raised when a connection is lost as errors that require
    the transaction to be restarted.
*/
